# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
    "base": [
        ("Why a play area needs a deeper base than a lawn",
         "<p>Excavation for a {city} play area has to account for three layers stacking on top of each other, the compacted rock, the shock pad, and the turf itself, which is why the hole goes noticeably deeper than a lawn conversion covering the same square footage. Shortchanging that depth calculation is what leaves a finished play surface sitting visibly higher than the grass around it, and that mismatch creates its own trip hazard right at the edge even when every layer above the rock was installed correctly. Working out the full stack's thickness on paper before any soil comes out avoids discovering the shortfall halfway through the job.</p>"),
        ("Washed rock and what it means for a shock pad's stability",
         "<p>The same washed, open-graded crushed rock or crushed concrete required under any {city} lawn goes under a shock pad too, and skipping the wash step causes a different problem here than it does under grass. Fines from unwashed fill binding into a crust don't just block drainage under a pad, they also create an uneven surface for the pad to bond to, which can leave a shock pad sitting slightly unlevel across a play area's footprint. A pad that isn't seated evenly performs inconsistently across the use zone, even if its rated thickness matches the equipment's fall height on paper.</p>"),
        ("Grading a play area's base toward a drainage point",
         "<p>A {city} play surface's base carries the same one to two percent grade toward a drainage point that any residential lawn does, compacted in lifts before the shock pad goes down on top. That slope matters more here than it does under grass, since a shock pad sitting in standing water softens and loses cushioning performance faster than a lawn's backing degrades from the same exposure. Getting the grade right under the rock, before the pad and turf ever go down, is what keeps the finished play surface draining the way it's designed to for years rather than months.</p>"),
        ("Keeping the sand under a play area able to drain",
         "<p>The state's turf rule bars compacting the subgrade under any synthetic surface, including a {city} playground system, so tightly that it stops the native sand from percolating water down and away. A play area's base gets built firm enough to support a shock pad and repeated impact from jumping and landing, but that firmness has to stop short of sealing off the sand beneath it. A base that's been over-compacted in the name of stability ends up trapping water at the surface instead, which is exactly the outcome the rule is written to prevent.</p>"),
        ("Why a play surface's base can't let water pool",
         "<p>Perforated turf backing passes water at more than thirty inches an hour, well ahead of a typical one- to three-inch {city} summer downpour, but that capacity only matters if the base and pad beneath it can move water at a similar pace rather than holding it at the surface. The state's rule against pooling and added runoff covers a play area exactly as it covers any other patch of turf on the property, and a base built to that standard is what keeps a shock pad from sitting in standing water after every afternoon storm, the exact condition that breaks down a pad's cushioning fastest.</p>"),
    ],
    "product": [
        ("Matching shock pad thickness to fall height",
         "<p>Sizing a shock pad correctly starts with one number: how far a child could actually drop from the tallest point on the equipment it surrounds, since a {city} play system's pad rating has to meet or exceed that specific figure rather than an average across the whole structure. A modest backyard swing set with a platform in the five- to six-foot range typically pairs with roughly an inch of rated cushioning, while an eight- to ten-foot commercial or daycare structure calls for a noticeably heavier system underneath. Buying a pad by thickness alone, without confirming its tested rating against the actual equipment going over it, skips the part of the spec that matters most.</p>"),
        ("What ASTM F1292 actually tests",
         "<p>ASTM F1292 is the standard test method behind any shock pad's fall-height rating, measuring how much a surfacing product cushions an impact within a playground's use zone rather than just how thick or soft the material feels by hand. A {city} homeowner or daycare operator comparing two pad products should ask for the F1292 test data behind each one's claimed rating, since two pads that look and feel similar can carry different tested fall-height numbers depending on their internal construction. The rating on a spec sheet is only as good as the test data supporting it.</p>"),
        ("Where rubber infill is actually allowed",
         "<p>Florida's turf standard keeps rubber and other synthetic infill off a {city} lawn, pet run, or putting green entirely, restricting those areas to silica sand, zeolite, or a coated sand instead. Equipment fall zones get a specific carve-out from that same rule: the ground directly beneath a swing set or climbing structure is allowed to use rubber or another engineered infill product, since that material does cushioning work the natural-material options weren't built for. The line between the two infill categories is drawn at the equipment's footprint itself, not at the edge of the play area as a whole, which matters if a project blends turf types across one yard.</p>"),
        ("The backing still has to drain under a shock pad",
         "<p>Even with a cushioned pad doing most of the impact-absorption work, the turf backing on a {city} play surface still has to meet the same drainage standard as any other turf product, passing water at over thirty inches an hour. A backing that traps water against the pad below it defeats the purpose of building a well-graded base underneath, since the pad would end up sitting in moisture regardless of how well the rock layer drains. Product selection for a playground job has to account for the pad and the backing working together, not just the pad's rating in isolation.</p>"),
        ("Asking for the tested match, not just an IPEMA label",
         "<p>IPEMA runs third-party testing and certification against the ASTM F1292 standard, and a certified product has been independently validated rather than simply sold by a company that belongs to the association. For a {city} project, the more useful question isn't whether a product carries an IPEMA mark at all, but whether that specific pad's tested rating actually covers the specific equipment's fall height being installed over it. A general certification on a brochure doesn't confirm that match on its own; the manufacturer's test documentation for that exact combination does.</p>"),
    ],
    "process": [
        ("Setting the containment edge before the base goes in",
         "<p>A {city} playground project starts with a containment edge, a curb, header board, or buried edging, set in place before any base material arrives, which fixes the play area's footprint while everything else gets built up inside it. Doing this first keeps rock, pad, and infill material from migrating into surrounding beds or lawn as the job progresses, rather than trying to contain loose material after the fact. Every later step, grading, compacting, and laying the pad, works within the boundary this edge establishes from the start.</p>"),
        ("Bonding the pad to the base against repeated impact",
         "<p>Once the compacted rock base is graded and firm, the shock pad on a {city} play surface typically gets glued or bonded directly to it, keeping the pad from shifting or creeping out of position under the repeated impact of children jumping and landing in the same spots day after day. A pad that isn't secured this way can work loose at its edges over time, even if its rated thickness was correct from day one, which is a maintenance problem distinct from any issue with the fall-height rating itself.</p>"),
        ("Trimming turf tight to the containment edge",
         "<p>Once the shock pad is bonded in place on a {city} project, turf goes over it seamed with tape and adhesive the same way it would on a lawn, but the trim line along the containment edge gets more attention here than anywhere else on the job. A wavy or loose cut at that boundary is a common shortcut on a rushed install, and while it doesn't change the pad's rated cushioning, it does leave a lip that can catch a small shoe or let infill wash out during the next storm. This trimming happens only after the pad is fully secured, never before.</p>"),
        ("Capping sprinklers before the play surface goes down",
         "<p>Any sprinkler head positioned to spray into a {city} play area's footprint gets capped off early in the build, since the statewide standard prohibits watering any synthetic surface through an in-ground system, and a shock pad sitting in unexpected moisture loses useful life faster than the turf around it does. This happens at the same stage a lawn conversion handles its own capping, well before rock and pad material make a buried line harder to reach. From that point on, rainfall and an occasional hose rinse are the only water the play surface sees.</p>"),
        ("The final walk-through before handing off a play surface",
         "<p>The last step on a {city} playground project is infilling at the manufacturer's specified rate, then walking the finished surface to check for flat, even coverage and confirm nothing shifted during the last stages of the build. This walk-through is where a crew catches a low spot, an uneven seam, or a containment edge that needs a final adjustment before calling the job finished. Skipping this check and assuming a play surface is ready just because the visible steps are done is how small problems make it to the first day of use instead of getting caught beforehand.</p>"),
    ],
    "risks": [
        ("Why heat matters more on a play surface than a lawn",
         "<p>Nothing about turf heat matters more than it does on a {city} play surface, since children spend real time barefoot on it, pressing their whole body weight into direct contact rather than just crossing it in shoes the way an adult walking a lawn would. Full sun in Central Florida can carry a synthetic surface to 120 to 150°F, occasionally higher, which reaches the point of genuine discomfort within seconds of skin contact. Cooling it down with a hose before playtime, or simply scheduling active play earlier in the day during peak summer, does more for safety here than any single product choice in the pad or turf itself.</p>"),
        ("The pad that looks right but isn't rated for the fall height",
         "<p>The single biggest danger on a {city} play surface hides completely from view: a shock pad rated for a shorter fall than the equipment actually sitting above it, a five-foot-rated pad under an eight-foot platform, for example, looks exactly like a correctly specified one once turf is rolled over the top. Nothing about that mismatch shows up during a normal walkthrough; it only becomes obvious when the pad is formally tested against its documentation, or, in the worst case, after an actual fall. Requesting the specific pad's tested rating matched to the exact equipment installed above it is the only reliable way to catch this before it matters.</p>"),
        ("Low-E glass facing a play area",
         "<p>Checking for low-emissivity glass near a {city} play area deserves the same attention as checking it anywhere else on a property with turf: a patio door or window built with that coating can throw back enough concentrated sunlight to soften polyethylene fiber, which begins to give around 175 to 200°F, a lower bar than most homeowners assume a mere reflection could reach. A scorched spot on equipment that otherwise sits in regular shade is the usual giveaway that this is happening rather than ordinary wear. Shifting the equipment's position or the play area's layout by a few feet typically resolves it without touching the turf or pad specification at all.</p>"),
        ("Drip lines, shade, and where a play area can sit",
         "<p>The same drip-line rule that keeps turf away from a live oak's roots applies to playground equipment, so a shaded spot under mature canopy in {city}, appealing for keeping a play surface cooler through summer, still needs a certified arborist's sign-off before excavation for the base and pad happens nearby. The extra excavation depth a playground system needs makes this more of a concern than it would be for grass alone, since digging four to six inches under canopy roots carries more risk than a shallow lawn conversion does.</p>"),
        ("What a swale-adjacent play area risks in a storm",
         "<p>A {city} play area built too close to a drainage swale, or within ten feet of a pond or canal without a seawall separating the two, runs into the same setback violations a lawn would, but the consequences compound faster here because a shock pad sitting in standing water softens and loses cushioning performance well before the turf itself shows any wear. A base that wasn't graded to keep water moving through and away leaves the pad soaked after every storm, shortening its rated life regardless of how correctly it was matched to the equipment's fall height at installation.</p>"),
    ],
    "care": [
        ("Clearing debris before it creates uneven footing",
         "<p>Leaves, acorns, and other debris on a {city} play surface need regular clearing, more urgently than on a lawn, since anything working down into the infill under active play can create an uneven spot right where a child is running or landing rather than just a cosmetic issue. This is a quick habit during a live oak's seasonal drop, when debris accumulates faster than on an open lawn without canopy overhead. A play area checked weekly during peak leaf-drop stays safer underfoot than one checked only during general yard cleanup.</p>"),
        ("Checking seams and the containment edge after storms",
         "<p>After heavy rain or wind, a {city} play surface's seams and containment edge are worth checking specifically, since anything that's lifted or opened creates both a trip hazard and a path for infill to wash out during the next storm. A small gap caught early is a quick repair; the same gap left through a second or third storm tends to widen and can undermine the pad's edge along with the turf. This check takes only a few minutes but matters more here than on a lawn, since a lifted edge on a play surface is a safety issue, not just a cosmetic one.</p>"),
        ("Why older play surfaces need their pad rechecked",
         "<p>Every shock pad loses some cushioning capacity year over year simply from being landed on and jumped on repeatedly, so a {city} play surface that's been in the ground for five years or longer deserves a check against the fall-height rating it originally carried rather than an assumption that nothing's changed. That kind of wear is invisible from the surface, since a compressed pad and a fresh one look identical once turf is rolled over both. Any time the equipment itself gets swapped for something taller, this check becomes even more important, since a pad matched correctly years ago may no longer clear the new fall height.</p>"),
        ("Rinsing a play surface before kids go out in summer",
         "<p>A hose rinse timed just before outdoor play begins can pull a {city} play surface's temperature down 30 to 50 degrees in a matter of minutes, and building that step into a summer routine matters here in a way it doesn't for a lawn nobody sits on directly. Rinsing ahead of an afternoon play window, rather than waiting until the surface already feels uncomfortably hot underfoot, keeps the worst of the afternoon heat from ever becoming an issue in the first place. It's one of the simplest habits on this list and among the most worth keeping consistent through the hottest months.</p>"),
        ("Watching infill stay inside the containment edge",
         "<p>Infill on a {city} play surface, whether it's the rubber allowed directly under equipment or the sand-based product elsewhere, gradually migrates toward and sometimes past the containment edge under regular foot traffic and rain. Checking for infill building up outside the edge, or thinning out inside it, catches both problems before they affect footing or drainage. Topping off thin spots and sweeping stray material back inside the boundary is a small habit that keeps the surface performing evenly across its entire footprint rather than just at the center.</p>"),
    ],
}
